/*!
    \page vscodeext-how-profile-qml.html
    \previouspage vscodeext-how-tos.html
    \nextpage vscodeext-reference.html

    \ingroup vscodeext-how-to-qml

    \title Profile QML code

    With QML Profiler, you can find causes for typical performance problems
    in Qt Quick applications, such as slowness and unresponsive, stuttering user
    interfaces.

    You can launch QML Profiler from \QVSC to profile a running QML application
    and to analyze profiling data.

    \section1 Install QML profiler

    To profile QML applications, you need to install QML Profile. Go to
    \uicontrol {Command Palette} and select
    \uicontrol {Qt: Install the most recent QML trace viewer}.

    If you do not install QML Profiler, \QVSC offers to install it the first time
    you start profiling a application.

    \section1 Collect profiling data

    To collect profiling data about the currently open application:

    \list 1
        \li Go to \uicontrol {Command Palette} and select
            \uicontrol {Qt: Profile QML application} or
            \inlineimage {profiler-start.png} {Profile QML application}
            (\uicontrol {Profile QML application}) on the toolbar.
        \li Use the application to collect profiling data.
        \li Close the application or select
            \inlineimage {profiler-stop.png} {Profile QML application}
            (\uicontrol {Stop QML profiler}) on the toolbar.
    \endlist

    Use the QML Profiler to collect data and analyze the results.

    \image {qml-profiler.webp} {QML Profiler}

    For more information, see \l {\QC: Profiling QML applications}
    {Profiling QML applications} in the \QC documentation.

    \section1 Attach QML Profiler to running applications

    To attach the QML Profiler to a running application for collecting profiling
    data:

    \list 1
        \li In the \uicontrol Terminal, start the application with the following
            arguments:
            \badcode
            <your_executable_path> -qmljsdebugger=host:<IP_address>,port:<port_number>,block,services:QmlProfiler,CanvasFrameRate
            \endcode
            Where \e IP_address is the IP address of the host where the
            application is running, \e port_number is the debugging port,
            and block prevents the application from running until the debug
            client connects to the server. This enables profiling from the start.
        \li When the application is running, go to \uicontrol {Command Palette},
            and select \uicontrol {Qt: Attach to running QML application for profiling}.
        \li Enter the IP address of the host, and then select \key Enter.
        \li Enter the port number, and then select \key Enter.
    \endlist
*/
